cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  CHM696 Cyclic Peptide Design: A Multi-Method Computational
  Pipeline for Evaluating Cyclic Peptide Binders to MDM2
message: >-
  If you use this pipeline or adapt it for teaching or research,
  please cite it using the metadata below.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Bryon
    family-names: Drown
    email: bsdrown@purdue.edu
    affiliation: >-
      Department of Chemistry, Purdue University,
      West Lafayette, IN, USA
    orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5631-512X'
repository-code: 'https://github.com/bdrown/chm696-cyclic-peptide-design'
url: 'https://github.com/bdrown/chm696-cyclic-peptide-design'
abstract: >-
  A multi-method computational pipeline for generating and
  evaluating cyclic peptide binders against a protein target,
  developed for CHM696 Chemical Biology at Purdue University.
  The pipeline uses MDM2 as an example target and compares three
  philosophically different scoring approaches: generative-model
  ranking (ODesign), ML-based structure prediction with interface
  confidence scoring (Boltz-2), and physics-based binding free
  energy calculation via molecular dynamics and MM-GBSA
  (AmberTools and OpenMM). The pedagogical goal is to expose
  students to the strengths and limitations of each approach by
  having them observe method disagreements on real candidate
  designs.
keywords:
  - computational chemistry
  - drug discovery
  - molecular dynamics
  - cyclic peptides
  - MDM2
  - protein-peptide interactions
  - MM-GBSA
  - AmberTools
  - OpenMM
  - Boltz-2
  - ODesign
  - chemical biology
  - education
  - Purdue University
